Constructor chaining is a technique in Java where one constructor is called from within another constructor of the same class. This is useful when you want to reuse the code from one constructor to another, especially when initializing common fields or when you have multiple constructors that share some common initialization logic.

When constructor chaining is used, the constructor that is being called is referred to as the "chained constructor". The chained constructor can be either the default constructor or any other constructor of the same class. The constructor being called is passed as an argument to the constructor being defined, using the "this" keyword.

Here is an example of constructor chaining in Java:

```java
public class Person {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = 0;
    }

    public Person(String name, int age) {
        this(name);
        this.age = age;
    }

    // getters and setters
}
```

In the example above, the constructor with two arguments is chaining to the constructor with one argument. The first line of the constructor with two arguments is calling the constructor with one argument using the "this" keyword. This allows the code in the constructor with two arguments to reuse the code in the constructor with one argument.

Constructor chaining can be used to simplify the code and make it more readable, especially when you have multiple constructors that share some common initialization logic. It can also help to reduce code duplication and make the code more maintainable.

