Here is an example of a React component that displays the current day of the week and date:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import moment from 'moment';

class CurrentDate extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {
      date: moment().format('dddd, MMMM Do YYYY')
    };
  }

  componentDidMount() {
    this.timerID = setInterval(
      () => this.tick(),
      60000
    );
  }

  componentWillUnmount() {
    clearInterval(this.timerID);
  }

  tick() {
    this.setState({
      date: moment().format('dddd, MMMM Do YYYY')
    });
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h1>Current Date</h1>
        <p>{this.state.date}</p>
      </div>
    );
  }
}

export default CurrentDate;
```

This component uses the `moment` library to format the current date and time. It also uses the `setInterval` function to update the date every minute. The component renders the current date and time in the format of "dddd, MMMM Do YYYY".

You can use this component in your React application by importing it and rendering it in your JSX. For example:

```javascript
