To add a Chrome frame to an HTML page, you need to use the `<iframe>` tag with the `srcdoc` attribute and the `--disable-web-security` flag. Here's an example of how to update your HTML code to add a Chrome frame:

```html
<head>
  <title>My website</title>
  <style>
    html, body, iframe {
      margin: 0;
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
    }
  </style>
  <script type="text/javascript">
    function openFrame() {
      var iframe = document.createElement('iframe');
      iframe.srcdoc = '<html><head><title>Chrome Frame</title><meta http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible" content="IE=edge,chrome=1"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"><style>body{margin:0;}</style></head><body style="margin:0;">Hello, this is a Chrome Frame!</body></html>';
      iframe.style.width = '100%';
      iframe.style.height = '100%';
      iframe.allowfullscreen = true;
      iframe.frameBorder = '0';
      document.body.appendChild(iframe);
      iframe.contentWindow.navigator.appVersion.includes('Chrome') && iframe.contentWindow.navigator.appVersion.includes('Edge') ? iframe.contentWindow.execCss('--disable-web-security') : null;
      window.addEventListener('message', function(event) {
        if (event.data === 'ready') {
          document.body.removeChild(iframe);
        }
      });
      iframe.contentWindow.postMessage('ready', '*');
    }
  </script>
</head>
<body onload="openFrame()">
</body>
```

This code creates an `<iframe>` element with the Chrome frame content and sets the width and height to 100%. It also includes the `--disable-web-security` flag for Chrome and Edge browsers. The `<script>` tag in the `<head>` section creates a function `openFrame()` that adds the `<iframe>` element to the page and sets up communication between the parent and child frames to remove the `<iframe>` element once the frame is ready. The `<body>` tag sets the `onload` event to call the `openFrame()` function when the page loads.

To make an AJAX query to get data from the given URL using JavaScript, you can use the `XMLHttpRequest` object or a library like jQuery. Here's an example using both methods:

**Using XMLHttpRequest:**

```javascript
const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('GET', 'https://www.arecipe.com/data');
xhr.onload = function() {
  if (xhr.status === 200) {
    const data = JSON.parse(xhr.responseText);
    // Process the data here
  } else {
    console.log('Error:', xhr.statusText);
  }
};
xhr.send();
```

**Using jQuery:**

```javascript
$.ajax({
  url: 'https://www.arecipe.com/data',
  type: 'GET',
  dataType: 'json',
  success: function(data) {
    // Process the data here
  },
  error: function(xhr, textStatus, errorThrown) {
    console.log('Error:', textStatus, errorThrown);
  }
});
```

Both examples make an asynchronous request to the given URL and process the response data when it is received. The main difference is that the jQuery example uses a simpler and more concise syntax.

